From deb2f816d32a6ee03053955175d9224186f32779 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 03:53:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 113/119] feat: rec_ImportHDRI - a measured HDRI becomes a
 probe with a known absolute luminance

There was no place a calibration could happen. The Resource Compiler applies no scale,
no gamma and no tone map anywhere on the HDR path - every preset is
colorspace=linear,linear and CubeMapGen is driven with Degamma 1.0, Scale 1.0 - it
cannot read .exr at all, and it parses and then discards Radiance's own EXPOSURE= and
PRIMARIES=. Whatever unit is in the file is the unit in the .dds, and nothing records
what that unit was.

So the tool writes exactly the artefact the environment-probe bake already writes - a
16-bit-float TIFF, six faces in a horizontal strip, carrying /preset=HDRCubemap_highQ -
and then calls the RC on it the way CEnvironmentProbeComponent::Generate() does.
Everything downstream (the GGX convolution, the 32^2 _diff sibling, BC6UH, the atlas,
the shader) is unchanged, and no engine, RC or shader code changed for this.

  rec_ImportHDRI <file.hdr|file.exr> <name> [--nits <sky cd/m2>] [--remove-sun]

- .exr through the same static tinyexr the EXR writer already links; its chromaticities
  attribute is read and ACES2065-1 / ACEScg are converted to linear Rec.709, because
  BC6UH is UNSIGNED and clips the negatives an AP0 environment carries. No attribute
  means Rec.709, which is what the spec says, said out loud in the log.
- .hdr through a Radiance RGBE reader written here (~120 lines: text header, -Y +X
  resolution line, flat and new-style per-channel RLE scanlines). The RC has a copy of
  rgbe.c but it lives in a separate tool binary this plugin cannot link. The file's
  EXPOSURE= is honoured rather than discarded.
- --nits N scales the image so the SKY - the MEDIAN luminance of the upper hemisphere,
  sun excluded - reads N cd/m2. A median, for the same reason the exposure meter uses a
  histogram. Without it the source values are taken to BE cd/m2, logged as the
  assumption it is.
- --remove-sun takes every texel above 50x the sky median, refuses if the region is
  larger than 0.01 sr (that is a sky, not a disc), measures its illuminance and
  direction, patches it with the surrounding sky's own colour, and prints the lux and
  the angles to type into the time of day. This is not optional physics: BC6UH tops out
  at 6.55e8 cd/m2, below the solar disc's 1.5e9, and a 256 face cannot resolve half a
  degree. The analytic sun re-adds it properly, with shadows and speculars the baked one
  never had.
- 4-16x supersampled equirect-to-cube, the sample count taken from the source's own
  width so an 8K panorama does not alias into a 256 face. The face directions are
  reproduced from CTexture::DrawSceneToCubeSide's own table and CreateOrientation call,
  so the face order, up vectors and rolls cannot drift from the renderer's.
- a <name>_rec_cm.probe.txt sidecar records source, primaries, scale, sky nits and the
  removed sun. It is the only record of what the numbers mean; a .tif cannot carry one.

docs/SceneReferredContent.md section 8, including the anchors (overcast sky ~2000 nits,
clear blue 5000-8000), the +Y-centred orientation convention, why the _rec suffix is a
small deliberate lie, and the warning never to drop a foreign pre-convolved irradiance
map in as _cm_diff (external tools emit E, the RC's cosine filter emits E/pi - a silent
1.65 stops).

Notes: scene-notes/decisions/s8-sky-and-probes.md part 2, research/s8-hdri-probes.md.

