+---
+
+## 8. HDRI — importing a measured environment as a probe
+
+`rec_ImportHDRI` turns an equirectangular `.hdr` or `.exr` into a CRYENGINE environment probe with
+a **known absolute luminance**. It exists because there is no other place the calibration can
+happen: the Resource Compiler applies no scale, no gamma and no tone map on the HDR path
+(`colorspace=linear,linear` everywhere), it cannot read `.exr` at all, and it parses and then
+discards Radiance's own `EXPOSURE=` and `PRIMARIES=` header fields. Whatever unit is in the file is
+the unit in the `.dds`, and nothing records what that unit was.
+
+```
+rec_ImportHDRI <file.hdr|file.exr> <name> [--nits <sky cd/m²>] [--remove-sun]
+```
+
+It writes `<assets>/textures/cubemaps/<name>_rec_cm.tif` — a 16-bit-float TIFF with the six cube
+faces in a horizontal strip, carrying `/preset=HDRCubemap_highQ`, which is *exactly* the artefact
+the environment-probe bake already produces — then runs the RC on it. The 256² specular cube and
+its 32² `_diff` sibling come out of that one call. Nothing in the engine, the RC or the shaders
+changes for any of this.
+
+### 8.1 The absolute scale
+
+| | what happens |
+|---|---|
+| **without `--nits`** | the source pixel values are taken to **be** cd/m². Right for a file you calibrated yourself; wrong, and loudly logged as an assumption, for a vendor HDRI |
+| **with `--nits N`** | the image is scaled so the **sky** — the median luminance of the upper hemisphere, with the sun excluded — reads N cd/m² |
+
+A median rather than a mean, for the same reason the exposure meter uses a histogram: one 10⁶ texel
+moves a mean and cannot move a median. Useful anchors: an overcast sky is roughly 2 000 cd/m², a
+clear blue sky away from the sun 5 000–8 000, and near the horizon at midday 15 000.
+
+A Radiance file that carries its own `EXPOSURE=` is honoured (the RC would have ignored it), and
+the factor is recorded.
+
+### 8.2 The sun has to come out
+
+BC6UH tops out at 65 504, which is 6.55 × 10⁸ cd/m² in engine units — **below the solar disc's
+1.5 × 10⁹** — and a 256² face cannot resolve a half-degree disc in any case. So a probe simply
+cannot hold a sun; leaving one in gives a clipped, hue-shifted, aliased smear.
+
+`--remove-sun` finds every texel above 50 × the sky median, checks the region is smaller than
+0.01 sr (a bright region larger than that is a sky or a big practical, and is left alone with a
+warning), measures it, replaces it with the surrounding sky's own colour, and prints:
+
+```
+[HDRI] SUN REMOVED: 118 400 lux, 0.00007 sr (14 texels), elevation 47.30 deg, azimuth -22.10 deg
+[HDRI]   -> put 118400 into the time of day's Sun Intensity and match the sun's angles.
+```
+
+Type that into the time of day. The **analytic** sun then re-adds the disc properly — in lux,
+π-correct on the ReC path, with real shadows and real specular highlights, which the baked one
+never had.
+
+### 8.3 Colour
+
+A probe must contain **linear Rec.709**, because BC6UH is unsigned: an AP0 or AP1 encoded
+environment carries values Rec.709 cannot represent without negatives, and the format clips those
+to zero. So the conversion is not optional and there is no pass-through mode.
+
+* `.exr`: the file's `chromaticities` attribute is read and classified. ACES2065-1 (AP0) and
+  ACEScg (AP1) are converted; anything else, and any file with no attribute, is treated as Rec.709
+  — which is what the OpenEXR specification says the default is. The verdict is in the log line.
+* `.hdr`: Radiance's `PRIMARIES=` is not read (it is rare and unreliable in practice); the file is
+  treated as Rec.709.
+
+Negative and non-finite samples are clipped to zero here, where the count is reported, instead of
+silently inside the RC.
+
+### 8.4 Orientation, resolution, and what the tool does not do
+
+The convention: the **centre of the image looks along +Y** (the engine's "north"), u increases
+eastward towards +X, the top row is the zenith. There is no yaw argument — rotate the source if the
+horizon needs turning.
+
+Faces are 256², supersampled from the source at 4–16 samples per axis chosen from the source's own
+width, so an 8K panorama does not alias into a 256 face. The residual is clamped at 10 000 engine
+units (10⁸ cd/m²), far above any sky and far below the format's ceiling, so it can only ever catch
+a sun the removal missed — and it says so when it fires.
+
+A `<name>_rec_cm.probe.txt` sidecar records the source, its primaries, the scale to engine units,
+the resulting sky luminance in nits and the sun that was removed. **It is the only record of what
+the numbers mean**; a `.tif` cannot carry one.
+
+The `_rec` suffix is emitted so the probe loader's convention check stays quiet. It is a small lie:
+an imported HDRI is convention-*independent* — it was never lit by our sun — but the alternative is
+a warning on every load of a correct asset.
+
+**Supply the environment, never a pre-convolved irradiance map.** External convolvers emit `E`; the
+RC's cosine-180 filter emits `E/π`, and dropping a foreign `_cm_diff.dds` in is a silent 1.65 stops.
